# Data Visualization With R

These tutorials build statistical graphics in R using [ggplot2](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/), the most widely used plotting package in the R ecosystem. Rather than demonstrating each chart type in isolation, the lessons follow a single analysis from first look to finished figure, using publicly available health and nutrition data.

The material is organized around the *grammar of graphics*: the idea that any statistical figure can be described as data mapped to visual properties, drawn with geometric shapes, and adjusted through scales and coordinate systems. Learning that grammar makes it possible to construct a figure that has never been seen before, rather than searching for an example to copy.

The later tutorials extend this into statistical modelling. Visualization is not applied to a model once the analysis is finished — it is how the data are explored before a model is chosen, how its assumptions are checked after it is fitted, and how its results are communicated. Those tutorials cover the models most common in biostatistics, using the same health and nutrition data.

## Who These Tutorials Are For

Readers should be comfortable with basic R syntax — assigning variables, calling functions, and working with data frames. No prior plotting experience is assumed. Researchers in the health sciences will recognize the datasets, but the techniques apply to any field.

For help installing R and RStudio, see [Installing R and RStudio for the First Time](../../ai/r/00-installing-r-and-rstudio.md).

## Data

Every lesson draws on the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES), a public-domain survey conducted by the United States Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Two small teaching datasets supplement it where a simpler example is clearer. [Setup and the Data](00-setup-and-data.md) describes all three, explains how the survey is structured, and covers the sampling issues that affect how its figures should be interpreted.

A parallel set of tutorials covers the [same material in Python](../python/index.md).

